; The FFI abort path, which was documented in four places and executed
; by nothing.
;
; `110-narrow-ints.ax` describes this abort in detail and then says why
; it cannot observe it: "the abort is a process-ending event, and a
; fixture that expects 0 cannot observe it". True of that fixture. Not
; true of the corpus - `check-ffi.sh` reads a `; expect N` trailer, and
; every one of the 35 cases said 0, so the whole abort path was prose.
; A shim that never aborted at all, or aborted with the wrong status,
; passed every gate in this repository.
;
; 256 DOES NOT FIT IN A `u8`. `byte_plus(b: u8, delta: i64)` takes its
; first argument through `TryFrom`, and an infallible call has no
; channel to report the failure through - so the boundary ends the
; process rather than truncating, because truncation was the measured
; silent-wrong-answer case (`narrow(4294967297)` on a `u32` printed 1).
;
; THE STATUS IS 73 AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS CASE. It was 72 until
; 2026-08-24, chosen because 72 "is what Axiom's own runtime traps
; use" - which is exactly what made it useless. `MM-EXEC-16` reserves
; 70 for allocation failure, 71 for an unhandled operation and 72 for
; division by zero, all three raised by code the Axiom compiler
; emitted. This one is raised on the other side of the boundary. A
; supervisor reading 72 in a log could not tell a peer's out-of-range
; length from a division by zero, and the remedies have nothing in
; common.
;
; The message on fd 2 always named the function and the argument. The
; status now matches it.
(import IO)

(import Demo)

;@axiom:effect(io)
(pub fn (main)
  ; NOT REACHED. `bytePlus` ends the process here, and the trailer
  ; below is the assertion - if the boundary ever starts answering
  ; instead of aborting, this line runs, the case exits 0, and the
  ; expected 73 says so.
  {
    (println "UNREACHABLE: the boundary accepted 256 as a u8")
    (bytePlus 256 1)
    0
  }
)
; expect 73
